  "doc": {
    "doi": "10.3140/bull.geosci.1383",
    "citation": "Le Hérissé, A., Paris, F. & Steemans, P. 2013. Late Ordovician–earliest Silurian palynomorphs from northern Chad and correlation with contemporaneous deposits of southeastern Libya. Bulletin of Geosciences 88(3), 483–504.",
    "language": "English",
    "reports_window_spores": true,
    "comment": "The main spore list is pooled across the Moussegouda core; Figure 10 also identifies specimens from individual levels. The sample records below overlap the pooled interval and must not be counted as independent assemblages without accounting for that overlap. The authors favour a Hirnantian age for the lower cores and latest Hirnantian for the upper cores, but cannot exclude earliest Rhuddanian for the latter. Older acritarchs are reworked and some chitinozoans may be reworked; spore reworking is not demonstrated. The study used old palynological slides previously studied by Calandra (SNEAP internal report, 1964), as well as new core samples. The two KW-2 samples yielded mixed palynomorphs, but no spore taxon is explicitly assigned to them in the text or Figure 10."
  },
